harvest your crops and pop your pimples  because we have scary stories to tell in  the dark yes if Auguste couldn't get any  scarier with the release of Dora the  Explorer we have Guillermo del Toro's  latest screenplay in this video we'll be  taking a deep dive into the ending and  explaining some of these badass monsters  along the way so here we go scary  stories begins on Halloween of 1968 in  the small town of Mill Valley  Pennsylvania and it's no wonder this  town is called Mill Valley as the local  paper mill is at the core of all the  spooky happenings spooky when local  teenagers Stella Oggy hormone and chuck  break into the abandoned bellows place a  house known for its child murders they  uncover the secret room of Sarah bellows  Sarah was the daughter of do dot bellows  the owner of the town's paper mill in  the late 1800s she was born with a rare  condition of albinism making her body  pale white and causing her family to  lock her in the basement away from the  local townspeople Sarah would spend her  time in the basement telling stories  through the wall to local children and  writing them in her book with her own  blood but Sarah held a darker secret she  knew that her father's paper mill was  responsible for the deaths of the  townspeople  mercury from the mill had spread into  the water and poisoned many of mill  valleys inhabitants so when Stella on  the gang take her book it unleashes the  full rage of Sarah bellows hell-bent on  destroying anything that comes in her  way the book begins to target one kid  per day writing a story in blood which  eventually comes true the first victim  is Tommy and a story simply titled  Harold named after the beautiful  scarecrow Tommy loves to beat the [ __ ]  out of her  as Harold comes to life he stabs Tommy  with a pitchfork eventually turning him  into his scarecrow himself ironic that  the thing Tommy hated is the thing that  he becomes and worst of all the game  finds out that the book can't be  destroyed or discarded when Stella  brings the book back to the mansion it  somehow ends up right back in her room  or when they decide to light it on fire  it emerges completely unscathed  the second victim is Augie whose story  is called the big toe a story told to  him by his father when he was a kid in  which a corpse  comes looking for it's missing big toe a  big toe that was in the stew he ate for  dinner he's captured by a character  simply known as the toe monster but by  the end of the film he's still missing  leaving room for a sequel the attack on  Oggy Spurs the gang to look into the  death of Sarah bellows they stop at  their local newspaper and dig up  articles from 1898 the year Sarah  apparently hung herself with her own  hair the papers reported that a servant  of the bellos Manor  Lulu Baptiste had taught Sarah black  magic which was used to kill the  townspeople but before they can check up  on Lulu the book begins to write its  next story the red spot referring to  Ruth's massive pimple that puts even my  worst high schools into shame  Ruth survives his attack after dozens of  spiders hatch out of her face  prompting the gang to hasten their  search for answers by questioning Lulu  Baptiste the bellows former servant who  apparently had taught Sarah black magic  they find Lulu but she's blind and  seemingly suffering from some form of  delirium nevertheless she tells them two  new pieces of key information that Sarah  was never taught black magic and that  she didn't hang herself at home as the  legend said rather at a psychiatric  hospital a psychiatric hospital Stella  and Ramon later visit and find out was  the location where Sarah was brutally  tortured via electroshock therapy her  family wanted her to admit that their  mill had nothing to do with the deaths  in town something that would be patently  false unfortunately it's also at this  hospital that the next story occurs the  dream a reference to Charlie's dream he  had in the Red Room while visited by a  pale lady and that's exactly what  happens  Charlie is consumed by the pea lady in a  room that's not only the color red but  the re d stands for records and  evaluation department the second-last  monster is the jangly man in a story  called NEET I go to Walker a lyric that  the monster sings in the printed version  of the story the jangly man is a  decomposing corpse that can stitch or  rip apart pieces of its body at will fun  fact the jangly man is performed by an  actual contortionist by the name of Troy  James who you may have seen on America's  Got Talent  the jangly man is after Ramone calling  him a coward as we learn earlier in the  story Ramone is on the run because he's  a draft dodger after his brother was  killed in Vietnam he decided to live out  of his car in order to avoid the draft  and that's one of the great things about  these monsters their stories all somehow  relate to the characters for example  Ramone whose brother was blown to pieces  is now literally chased by a creature  who is in pieces or Ruth a woman focused  on looking her best is scarred by a  spider-infested pimple the monsters have  become reality because of the stories  the characters learned as children for  example chuck's comes from a dream  Auggie a story his dad told him and  Ramon a campfire story he was told as a  child with limited time left  Ramon distracts the jangly man while  Stella goes after Sara in the abandoned  house if she can tell Sara that they  know she was innocent perhaps they can  put an end to her madness and that's  exactly what happens the final story the  haunted house sees Stella confront the  final monster Sara still a tells her  that she'll make sure Sara's story gets  out there that the truth of her  innocence and what her family did will  be revealed  Sara hands her an ink pen and makes her  write the story in blood  because who doesn't like a story written  in good old-fashioned blood and with  that Sara's soul is put to rest and the  jangly man is destroyed along with it  Sara has redeemed herself and learned  from Stella that the rage she projected  onto innocent people is no different  than how her family dealt with her even  though she was innocent the film ends  with Stella saying goodbye to Ramon who  has made the decision to fight in  Vietnam his character arc was one of  coward to hero his fight with the jangly  man has allowed him to muster the  courage to fight abroad rather than run  away from the thing that scared him the  most  similarly Stella has also changed at the  beginning of the film she's asked by  Oggy if she's going to hand in her  stories to the school paper but she  doesn't because she's not confident in  her own writing at the end we see her  win multiple awards for her work and she  tells us that if anything Sara taught  her never to give up and never giving up  is where the story ends with Stella Ruth  and her father venturing away from Mill  Valley to find a way to save Oggy and  Chuck paving the way for an eventual  sequel but  period of 1968 is also very interesting  because of its social commentary  sixty-eight was a time of great change  and so to do our characters go through  their own big changes it was a time  filled with monsters of its own kind  like Nixon who tried to hide the truth  about Vietnam and Watergate from the  American people just like the bellows  family tried to hide the truth about  their poisonous paper mill from the  community scary stories although not  really bringing anything new to the  genre is a fun well-executed horror I  want to hear your thoughts below and  make sure to LIKE and subscribe I got  more videos on the way and you can  follow me on twitter at think story  whitey and until next time remember  daddy loves you very much
 

